## Proof

**Yes, it is possible.**

**Construction.** Tile the unit square with a $1992 \times 1992$ grid of congruent smaller squares. Each small square has side length

$$\frac{1}{1992}.$$

There are $1992^2$ such small squares. They are non-overlapping (they form a standard grid partition) and their union is exactly the unit square, so they all fit inside it.

**Verification of the sum.** The sum of the side lengths of all the small squares is

$$1992^2 \cdot \frac{1}{1992} = 1992.$$

This equals the required value. $\blacksquare$

